ape -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error paper.tex\n\n## Supplementary Information validation\n- appendix_present: `True`\n- heading_present: `True`\n- placed_after_references: `True`\n- pre_bibliography_clearpage_present: `True`\n- clearpage_present: `True`\n- visible_character_count: `6129`\n- substantive_element_count: `9`\n- rendered_checked: `False`\n- rendered_heading_present: `False`\n- rendered_visible_character_count: `0`\n\n## Evidence validation\n- bundle_path: `work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/paper/scientific-evidence`\n- bundle_present: `True`\n- verification_report: `work/results/r03/claude/20261111_111139/paper/scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- total_references: `52`\n- resolved_references: `17`\n- anchored_references: `35`\n- dangling_references: `0`\n- manifests_checked: `3`\n\n## Agentic evidence audit\n- checked: `True`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `4`\n- summary: Every number, count, and the qualified final outcome match claims_grounding.json, paper_data.json, and the evaluator source. Four wording overclaims remain: two in Discussion, one in Results and Conclusion, and one in Related Work.\n\n### Audit findings to fix\n- `perm-control-erases-overclaim` (Discussion and Limitations (What is and is not shown)): The retained evidence supports only an average nRMSE gap of 0.678 between permuted-control and matched real episodes, which cover three of the 14 episodes per seed. No control nRMSE, control skill against column mean, or comparison of controls with the column-mean baseline was retained, so the evidence does not show that the control removes the whole improvement.\n  - claim: the improvement is not an artefact of the imputer's marginals since permuting the observations erases it\n  - evidence: paper_data.json final_evaluation.metrics.final_permuted_control_gap=0.6775 (the only control metric); claims_grounding.json claim[5] states only \"Permuted-control gap is 0.678 nRMSE\"; paper.tex Results paragraph \"Control\" already says the gap is an absolute difference to be read against 0.562\n  - requested_fix: Replace \"erases it\" with \"raises nRMSE by 0.678 on average over the three matched real episodes, so the predictions use the new model's own scores\". Do not say the control removes the gain over column means.\n- `unavoidable-residual-overclaim` (Discussion and Limitations (Practical reading)): 0.638 is the measured nRMSE of one selector-imputer pair at k=20 on 37 held-out MTEB models. Nothing in the evidence shows it is an irreducible floor, and the paper itself says no ablation, resampling interval, or alternative imputer comparison was run.\n  - claim: an unavoidable residual of about 0.64 history standard deviations remains on MTEB at k=20\n  - evidence: claims_grounding.json claim[3] supports only the nRMSE values at k=2, 5, 10, 20; paper_data.json final.metric.final_nrmse_mteb_k20=0.6377; paper.tex Limitations \"Unperformed analyses\" and \"Uncertainty\" paragraphs\n  - requested_fix: Change \"unavoidable residual\" to \"a residual error of about 0.64 history standard deviations for the evaluated pair on MTEB at k=20, with no claim about a lower bound\".\n- `greedy-vs-random-inference-too-strong` (Results (Selection versus imputation) and Conclusion): The paper derives this only from the difference between two clipped, hierarchically averaged skills (0.099 vs 0.016). Skill is a mean of per-episode ratios, and the paper says it does not equal one minus a ratio of pooled errors. The per-baseline nRMSE values were not retained. claims_grounding.json grounds only an \"indicative only\" error ratio of about 1.09. The flat statements are firmer than that grounding.\n  - claim: the greedy-forward baseline had larger error on average than the random-k baseline ... under a fixed ridge imputer, a leave-one-out greedy selector did not beat random subsets; Conclusion: a leave-one-out greedy selector can fail to beat random subsets\n  - evidence: claims_grounding.json claim[4] (\"implied error ratio about 1.09 is indicative only\"); paper.tex Results \"Selection versus imputation\" (\"per-baseline errors were not retained\") and \"Skill is a mean of ratios\" paragraph; evaluate_final.py computes skill as clip(1-cand/b) per episode, then averages\n  - requested_fix: Reword each statement as indicative only. For example, say \"the averaged skills suggest, but per-baseline errors were not retained to confirm, that the greedy-forward ridge baseline was no better than random-k ridge\". Remove the unqualified \"did not beat random subsets\".\n- `related-work-unsupported-details` (Related Work (Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks) and Introduction): references_provenance.json retains only the titles and metadata of these references, and claims_grounding.json grounds them at the level of \"sparse benchmark distillation\" and \"agreement depends on methodological choices\". The specifics (six benchmarks, thousands of models, recovery check, dozens of benchmarks) have no retained source text, so they are unsupported by the evidence trail.\n  - claim: metabench distills six benchmarks into a sparse one using the responses of thousands of models and checks recovery of the original scores; Introduction: agreement analyses across dozens of benchmarks\n  - evidence: references_provenance.json entries for Kipnis2024Metabench (title \"metabench - A Sparse Benchmark of Reasoning and Knowledge in Large Language Models\") and Perlitz2024BenchBench contain no abstract or snippet; claims_grounding.json claims for the item-level and agreement citations\n  - requested_fix: Cut the quantitative and procedural details to what the titles and grounded claims support. For example, say \"metabench distills a sparse benchmark from several benchmarks\" and \"agreement analyses across benchmarks\".\n# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. The draft is well supported overall: the methods (EM, stacking, NNLS, submodular selection, ridge), item-level cheaper-evaluation work, and latent-capability work are all cited, and the quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json`. Three targeted gaps remain. No retrieval was run in this stage.\n\n## Targets\n\n1. **related-work-comparators** (Related Work, high, comparator)\n   - Anchor: \"Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion ... are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices\"; \"here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark\".\n   - The closest prior work predicts unrun benchmark scores from a few observed ones, applies low-rank or matrix-completion methods to LLM score matrices, or selects what to run adaptively. The draft cites only generic matrix-completion sources and item-level work. The paper's differentiation claim is therefore not positioned against its direct competitors.\n   - Search topics: predicting LLM performance on unseen benchmarks from a benchmark subset; matrix completion or low-rank factorization of model-by-benchmark matrices; adaptive or IRT-based active selection for LLM evaluation.\n\n2. **intro-benchmark-redundancy-claim** (Introduction, medium, positioning)\n   - Anchor: \"are known to overlap in what they measure\".\n   - The premise that motivates budgeted evaluation is cited only to the suites themselves, which do not document redundancy.\n   - Search topics: correlation or redundancy among LLM benchmarks; low-dimensional structure in benchmark scores.\n\n3. **data-open-llm-leaderboard-context** (Method, protocol data paragraph, medium, benchmark_context)\n   - Anchor: \"the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks; 3371 history and 300 held-out models\".\n   - The leaderboard is one of three sealed evaluation datasets, and its six component benchmarks are named without citations. HELM and MTEB are cited.\n   - Search topics: Open LLM Leaderboard v2 description; IFEval, BBH, GPQA, MuSR and MMLU-Pro papers.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Cross-benchmark score prediction and matrix completion on LLM evaluation matrices\n- Adaptive or IRT-based test and benchmark selection for LLM evaluation\n- Open LLM Leaderboard and its component benchmarks\n- Empirical evidence of benchmark redundancy\n\n## Deliberately not flagged\n- Rank-Gauss transform and kNN: standard components, and the draft already states them as such.\n- Numeric results and protocol facts: grounded in evidence files.\n- Limitations and discussion text: deliberately self-critical, with no external claims that need support.\npostdraft_citation_weave_agent_claude_20261111_122649.log\npostdraft_citation_weave_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_122649.log\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\npostdraft_reference_retrieval.log\npostdraft_references.bib\npostdraft_references_provenance.json","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"751185a9-3d59-4708-bf91-8846160bf68b","uuid":"92ab247a-274a-4c13-9d2d-1993b2dc865b","timestamp":"2026-11-11T13:12:47.209Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"VENUE.md\nanalysis\nclaims_grounding.json\nconcept_figure_run.log\ndraft_reference_gaps.json\ndraft_reference_gaps.md\ndraft_reference_gaps_agent_claude_20261111_122649.log\ndraft_reference_gaps_prompt.md\ndraft_reference_gaps_validation_feedback.json\ndraft_reference_gaps_validation_feedback.md\nfigure_manifest.json\nfigures\nglobal_memory\nglobal_memory_recent.json\nglobal_memory_summary.md\nmemory.md\npaper.aux\npaper.bbl\npaper.blg\npaper.bst\npaper.fls\npaper.log\npaper.out\npaper.sty\npaper.tex\npaper_agent_claude_20261111_122649.log\npaper_data.json\npaper_diagnostics\npaper_diagnostics_20261111_122649_37076.log\npaper_events_20261111_122649.jsonl\npaper_evidence_audit.json\npaper_evidence_audit.md\npaper_evidence_audit_agent_claude_20261111_122649.log\npaper_evidence_audit_agent_claude_20261111_122649