{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BsPXZCwMqjFwqrJboGTvXn","type":"tool_result","content":"40:A full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB} are known to overlap in what they measure: agreement analyses across dozens of benchmarks and low-dimensional capability models fitted to public evaluations both point to strongly related scores \\citep{Perlitz2024BenchBench,Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}. This suggests a budgeted-evaluation protocol: an evaluator holding a historical matrix with $M$ models, $N$ benchmarks and missing cells receives a new model, can afford only $k\\ll N$ benchmark runs, and wants to infer the scores on the other $N-k$ benchmarks as accurately as possible. Two decisions then matter: \\emph{which} $k$ benchmarks to run, and \\emph{how} to turn their scores into predictions. A practitioner also needs to know how large $k$ must be.\n44:\\paragraph{Research question and hypotheses.} When only $k$ benchmarks can be run on a new model, how accurately can a history-fitted selector--imputer pair recover its other scores, how does the error fall with $k$, and how much of the gain comes from cross-benchmark structure versus the choice of which $k$ to run? We examine four hypotheses.\n61:\\caption{Cold-start protocol. A history matrix with missing cells is given to the method; it commits to $k$ benchmark indices; only the new model's scores on those $k$ benchmarks are revealed; the method predicts all other benchmarks; error is measured in units of history column standard deviations and compared with column-mean, random-$k$ ridge and greedy-forward ridge baselines and with permuted-observation controls.}\n68:\\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks.} tinyBenchmarks, anchor points and efficient-benchmarking studies shrink the number of items of a single benchmark while preserving its score or ranking \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}. metabench distills six benchmarks into a sparse one using the responses of thousands of models and checks recovery of the original scores \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}. Fluid benchmarking goes further by choosing items adaptively under an item-response model of latent ability \\citep{Hofmann2025Fluid}. These works reduce or adapt item counts within a benchmark or a fixed suite; here the unit of selection is a whole benchmark, the choice is committed before any score of the new model is seen, and the target is every benchmark not run.\n95:\\textbf{Proposed pair.} The selector--imputer pair of Section~\\ref{sec:impl}, selected on the development partition as the best-scoring configuration. The sealed evaluation ran either this pair or its latent-skill extension (below); the two have identical development scores, the recorded hash of the evaluated source tree matches neither retained source copy, and the available evidence cannot say which ran. Statements about the ``evaluated pair'' therefore refer to this selector--imputer family. \\textbf{Variant under search.} A simpler pair: z-scored columns, EM Gaussian, covariance-trace greedy selection with a coverage weighting, and the ridge-regularised Gaussian conditional mean, with shrinkage, factor rank and ridge strength tuned by history cross-validation. A third pair adds a monotone-link latent-skill member to the stack and a latent A-optimal selector candidate. The proposed pair and the simpler pair differ in several components at once (transform, selector candidates, stack, clipping), so their comparison (Table~\\ref{tab:search}) is a comparison of configurations, not an ablation. \\textbf{Evaluator baselines.} (a) \\emph{Column mean}: predict each benchmark's history mean. (b) \\emph{Random-$k$ ridge}: five random $k$-subsets (seeded from the dataset, $k$ and draw index), ridge imputer fitted on a rank-5 SVD-completed history (40 completion iterations), ridge strength chosen by leave-one-out over four values; the mean $\\nrmse$ over draws is the baseline. (c) \\emph{Greedy-forward ridge}: forward selection (up to 20 benchmarks) minimising leave-one-out ridge error on the remaining benchmarks of the completed history, with the same ridge imputer. Both ridge baselines share their imputer, so they probe selection with the imputer fixed; the proposed pair changes both selector and imputer.\n109:\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks, namely IFEval \\citep{Zhou2023IFEval}, BBH \\citep{Suzgun2023BBH}, MATH Level~5 \\citep{Hendrycks2021MATH}, GPQA \\citep{Rein2023GPQA}, MuSR \\citep{Sprague2023MuSR} and MMLU-Pro \\citep{Wang2024MMLUPro}; 3371 history and 300 held-out models, the held-out set capped at 300), and HELM Lite v1.13.0 core scenarios (ten scenarios; 66 history and 25 held-out models). Models are grouped by normalised name, family key and content hash (union-find); groups, never single models, are assigned to history or held-out, so no family or duplicated record appears on both sides. Held-out groups are drawn in hash order until at least a quarter of rows are held out. A separate MTEB partition with disjoint groups was used for model development (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n122:\\caption{Sealed evaluation on family-disjoint held-out models. (a) MTEB $\\nrmse$ against budget $k$ (mean over three seeds). (b) $\\nrmse$ per leaderboard (OLL: Open LLM Leaderboard) and pooled over the three. (c) Skill against column-mean, greedy-forward ridge and random-$k$ ridge baselines, pooled over leaderboards. No error bars: the retained metrics are seed means, and the three seeds share the same held-out models.}\n144:Skill vs.\\ greedy-forward ridge & 0.099 & selector+imputer jointly\\\\\n160:\\paragraph{Selection versus imputation.} Skill against random-$k$ ridge is only 0.016: at the pooled level the proposed pair has a mean relative error 1.6\\% lower than five random subsets imputed by a ridge model on a completed history. Skill against greedy-forward ridge is 0.099. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_random_selection]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_greedy_ridge]]\n161:Both figures mix selector and imputer, so neither isolates ``which $k$''. The two baselines share an imputer and differ only in selection; since skill against greedy ridge exceeds skill against random-$k$ ridge by 0.083, the greedy-forward baseline had larger error on average than the random-$k$ baseline in these evaluations. This is an inference from two averaged skills (the per-baseline errors were not retained), and it is the only retained evidence about selection alone: under a fixed ridge imputer, a leave-one-out greedy selector did not beat random subsets. For H3 the grounded conclusion is therefore modest. The pair's accuracy over a ridge imputer does not separate into a selector effect and an imputer effect, and the small margin over random-$k$ suggests that on these leaderboards most of the achievable gain over column means is available with a reasonable imputer and an arbitrary subset.\n167:\\paragraph{Skill is a mean of ratios.} Each skill value is computed per episode and then averaged along the hierarchy of Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}, and is clipped to $[-1,1]$. It is therefore not equal to one minus the ratio of pooled errors. If it were, the two ridge-baseline skills would imply that the greedy-forward baseline has an error about $(1-0.016)/(1-0.099)\\approx 1.09$ times that of the random-$k$ baseline. We read this only as indicative of the sign and rough size of the effect. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_random_selection]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.final_skill_vs_greedy_ridge]]\n195:\\paragraph{What is and is not shown.} The sealed result supports a bounded statement: on family-disjoint held-out models from three pinned leaderboards, a history-fitted selector--imputer pair predicts unrun benchmark scores with a mean relative error reduction (skill) of 0.434 against column means, and the improvement is not an artefact of the imputer's marginals since permuting the observations erases it. The evidence does not show that cross-benchmark structure is \\emph{exploited by selection}; it shows that observed scores help, and that a random subset with a ridge imputer comes within a skill of 0.016 of the pair.\n207:\\paragraph{Practical reading.} For an evaluator with a comparable history, a handful of well-covered benchmarks and a reasonable imputer already remove a large part of the column-mean error, further benchmarks buy progressively less, and an unavoidable residual of about 0.64 history standard deviations remains on MTEB at $k{=}20$.\n210:We posed the cold-start question of how well $k$ benchmark runs predict the remaining scores of a model from an unseen family, and evaluated a rank-Gauss, EM-Gaussian, wrapper-selected, NNLS-stacked selector--imputer family (one member of it, not identifiable from the retained evidence) on three leaderboards with family-disjoint held-out models. The sealed evaluation completed and qualified. Pooled nRMSE is 0.562 and skill against column means is 0.434, with a clear gap to permuted controls; on MTEB the error decreases from 0.787 to 0.638 as $k$ rises from 2 to 20 with sharply diminishing returns, and error levels differ across leaderboards. The margin over random-$k$ selection with a ridge imputer is only 0.016 and cannot be assigned to selection or imputation, and the retained evidence suggests that a leave-one-out greedy selector can fail to beat random subsets. The main unresolved questions are the isolated value of selection, uncertainty over held-out models, and behaviour beyond these three leaderboards; each needs additional controlled experiments that this study did not perform.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"751185a9-3d59-4708-bf91-8846160bf68b","uuid":"cff5fd20-bc5a-4dc9-8aab-befc75d203a3","timestamp":"2026-11-11T13:12:53.489Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"40:A full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB} are known to overlap in what they measure: agreement analyses across dozens of benchmarks and low-dimensional capability models fitted to public evaluations both point to strongly related scores \\citep{Perlitz2024BenchBench,Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}. This suggests a budgeted-evaluation protocol: an evaluator holding a historical matrix with $M$ models, $N$ benchmarks and missing cells receives a new model, can afford only $k\\ll N$ benchmark runs, and wants to infer the scores on the other $N-k$ benchmarks as accurately as possible. Two decisions then matter: \\emph{which} $k$ benchmarks to run, and \\emph{how} to turn their scores into predictions. A practitioner also needs to know how large $k$ must be.\n44:\\paragraph{Research question and hypotheses.} When only $k$ benchmarks can be run on a new model, how accurately can a history-fitted selector--imputer pair recover its other scores, how does the error fall with $k$, and how much of the gain comes from cross-benchmark structure versus the choice of which $k$ to run? We examine four hypotheses.\n61:\\caption{Cold-start protocol. A history matrix with missi