{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwCWqqVzw69DijfwBeaD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Rmm9huqeUsvEgB43kKGtRV","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'E'\np='paper.tex'\ns=open(p).read()\nreps=[\n(\"agreement analyses across dozens of benchmarks and low-dimensional\",\"agreement analyses across benchmarks and low-dimensional\"),\n(\"metabench distills six benchmarks into a sparse one using the responses of thousands of models and checks recovery of the original scores \\\\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}\",\"metabench distills a sparse benchmark from several benchmarks \\\\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}\"),\n(\"the greedy-forward baseline had larger error on average than the random-$k$ baseline in these evaluations. This is an inference from two averaged skills (the per-baseline errors were not retained), and it is the only retained evidence about selection alone: under a fixed ridge imputer, a leave-one-out greedy selector did not beat random subsets.\",\n \"the averaged skills suggest, but cannot confirm because per-baseline errors were not retained, that the greedy-forward ridge baseline was no better than the random-$k$ ridge baseline. This indicative inference is the only retained evidence about selection alone, and it does not establish that a leave-one-out greedy selector is worse than random subsets under a fixed ridge imputer.\"),\n(\"and the improvement is not an artefact of the imputer's marginals since permuting the observations erases it.\",\"and permuting the observations raises nRMSE by 0.678 on average over the matched real episodes, so the predictions use the new model's own scores. The retained evidence does not report control skill against column means.\"),\n(\"and an unavoidable residual of about 0.64 history standard deviations remains on MTEB at $k{=}20$.\",\"and a residual error of about 0.64 history standard deviations remains for the evaluated pair on MTEB at $k{=}20$, with no claim about a lower bound.\"),\n(\"and the retained evidence suggests that a leave-one-out greedy selector can fail to beat random subsets.\",\"and the averaged skills only suggest, without per-baseline errors to confirm, that a leave-one-out greedy ridge baseline was no better than random subsets.\"),\n]\nfor a,b in reps:\n    assert a in s,a[:40]\n    s=s.replace(a,b)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nE\ngrep -n \"Kipnis\\|Perlitz2024Bench\" claims_grounding.json | head; grep -n -i \"dozens\\|thousands\" paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":980,"cache_read_input_tokens":52275,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":980},"output_tokens":30,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"751185a9-3d59-4708-bf91-8846160bf68b","uuid":"ff5e867a-6368-4ab9-8c06-6674388a702b","timestamp":"2026-11-11T13:13:03.106Z","request_id":"req_011CgwCWokzsDS9Unn6yih3p"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rmm9huqeUsvEgB43kKGtRV","type":"tool_result","content":"119:        \"Kipnis2024Metabench\",\n131:        \"Perlitz2024BenchBench\",\n40:A full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB} are known to overlap in what they measure: agreement analyses across benchmarks and low-dimensional capability models fitted to public evaluations both point to strongly related scores \\citep{Perlitz2024BenchBench,Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}. This suggests a budgeted-evaluation protocol: an evaluator holding a historical matrix with $M$ models, $N$ benchmarks and missing cells receives a new model, can afford only $k\\ll N$ benchmark runs, and wants to infer the scores on the other $N-k$ benchmarks as accurately as possible. Two decisions then matter: \\emph{which} $k$ benchmarks to run, and \\emph{how} to turn their scores into predictions. A practitioner also needs to know how large $k$ must be.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"751185a9-3d59-4708-bf91-8846160bf68b","uuid":"4793b6e7-c7ef-4870-b715-f884567bd9e2","timestamp":"2026-11-11T13:13:03.226Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"119:        \"Kipnis2024Metabench\",\n131:        \"Perlitz2024BenchBench\",\n40:A full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB} are known to overlap in what they measure: agreement analyses across benchmarks and low-dimensional capability models fitted to public evaluations both point to strongly related scores \\citep{Perlitz2024BenchBench,Ruan2024ObservationalScaling,Polo2024Sloth}. This suggests a budgeted-evaluation protocol: an evaluator holding a historical matrix with $M$ models, $N$ benchmarks and missing cells receives a new model, can afford only $k\\ll N$ benchmark runs, and wants to infer the scores on the other $N-k$ benchmarks as accurately as possible. Two decisions then matter: \\emph{which} $k$ benchmarks to run, and \\emph{how} to turn their scores into predictions. A practitioner also needs to know how large $k$ must be.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
