--- final message ---
I fixed the four evidence-audit overclaims in `paper.tex`. I did not rerun the agentic audit, so I can't say it now passes.

| Audit finding | Change |
|---|---|
| Permuted control "erases" the improvement | Now says only that permuting the observations raises nRMSE by 0.678 on average over the matched real episodes. It also says control skill against column means was not retained. |
| "Unavoidable residual" of 0.64 on MTEB at k=20 | Now "a residual error of about 0.64 … for the evaluated pair", with no lower-bound claim. |
| Greedy selector "did not beat" random subsets (Results and Conclusion) | Reworded as indicative only. The averaged skills suggest the greedy-forward ridge baseline was no better than random-k ridge, but per-baseline errors were not retained to confirm it. |
| Unsupported specifics on metabench and BenchBench | Cut to "a sparse benchmark from several benchmarks" and "agreement analyses across benchmarks". |

I also softened the introduction's premise that suites "are known to overlap". It now says they are "plausibly overlapping", and that the agreement analyses and low-dimensional models "bear on how related benchmark scores are".

I reworded two matching claims in `claims_grounding.json` (the item-level reduction claim and the benchmark-agreement claim). It still parses as valid JSON.

The local compile gives 13 pages with 0 undefined references. The evidence verifier reports 52 references and 0 dangling.

The earlier docker compile failure was an environment problem, not a LaTeX error. I only compiled locally, so the docker compile step still needs to succeed in your pipeline.

The citation-enrichment targets (benchmark-level comparators, redundancy, Open LLM Leaderboard benchmarks) were handled in an earlier pass. The Open LLM Leaderboard component benchmarks and one adaptive-selection reference were added. The benchmark-level comparator gap is still open: retrieval hit HTTP 429, and the bibliography is at the 30-reference cap. The Related Work text states that latent-factor and matrix-completion predictors were not run as baselines.

The repair is logged in `memory.md` and `global_memory/`.
